<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deerfield, Massachusetts</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deerfield is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> valley of western Massachusetts, settled by English colonists in the 1670s. It was a frontier outpost in the wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, and on 29 February 1704 a French and Native raiding party destroyed much of the village, killing 56 people and marching more than 100 captives to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Some of the captives stayed permanently among the Mohawk at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deerfield-massachusetts.jpg" alt="Deerfield, Massachusetts" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jsayre64 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deerfield_MA_post_office.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
